<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiankang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">229–<a href="/years/589">589</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Jianye</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jiankang was the capital of the southern Chinese dynasties on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>. Founded as Jianye, capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a>'s state of Eastern Wu from 229, it was renamed Jiankang in 313 and served as the seat of the Eastern Jin and of the Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, Southern Qi, Liang and Chen dynasties until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> conquest of 589. It grew into one of the largest cities in the world and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and aristocratic culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiankang.jpg" alt="Jiankang" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jinling Ancient and Modern Map Research&quot; written by Chen Yi in the Ming Dynasty. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Southern_Dynasties_map_of_Nanjing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sima Rui, Prince of Langye, had been posted to <b>Jianye</b> in 307 on the advice of the aristocrat Wang Dao, and had won the cooperation of the gentry families of the lower Yangtze.</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 589 Sui soldiers entered <b>Jiankang</b>, the last Chen emperor surrendered, and the city was razed while its nobles were escorted north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The general Chen Baxian, who had defeated Hou Jing, gained control of <b>Jiankang</b> and in 557 accepted the abdication of the boy Emperor Jing of Liang, founding Chen with himself as Emperor Wu.</blockquote><a href="/state/chen-dynasty">Chen dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>He declared himself emperor in 229, with his capital at <b>Jianye</b> (Nanjing).</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-wu">Eastern Wu</a></li><li><blockquote>In 402 he marched on <b>Jiankang</b>, overthrowing the regent Sima Daozi's family, and in 403 he forced Emperor An of the Eastern Jin to abdicate and proclaimed himself emperor of Chu.</blockquote><a href="/state/huan-chu">Huan Chu</a></li><li><blockquote>In 417 the Eastern Jin general Liu Yu captured Chang'an, and the last ruler, Yao Hong, was executed at <b>Jiankang</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a></li></ul></section>
